tion(n_samples=100000, n_features=20,\n                                    n_informative=2, n_redundant=2)\n\ntrain_samples = 100  # Samples used for training the models\n\nX_train = X[:train_samples]\nX_test = X[train_samples:]\ny_train = y[:train_samples]\ny_test = y[train_samples:]\n\n# Create classifiers\nlr = LogisticRegression()\ngnb = GaussianNB()\nsvc = LinearSVC(C=1.0)\nrfc = RandomForestClassifier()\nax1 = plt.subplot2grid((3, 1), (0, 0), rowspan=2)\nax2 = plt.subplot2grid((3, 1), (2, 0))\n\n```\nHint: Search `examples/calibration/plot_compare_calibration.py` to get the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the calibration module structure:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['sklearn/calibration']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/calibration.py:_CalibratedClassifier.__init__\nsklearn/calibration.py:_CalibratedClassifier.calibrators_\nsklearn/calibration.py:_CalibratedClassifier\nsklearn/calibration.py:_fit_calibrator\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_multiclass.py:imports:6\nsklearn/calibration.py:CalibratedClassifierCV\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:impl:4\nexamples/calibration/plot_calibration_curve.py:plot_calibration_curve\nsklearn/calibration.py:_CalibratedClassifier.predict_proba\nsklearn/calibration.py:CalibratedClassifierCV.predict_proba\n##Searching for term \"sklearn/calibration\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==============================\n  3 | Probability Calibration curves\n  4 | ==============================\n  5 | \n  6 | When performing classification one often wants to predict not only the class\n  7 | label, but also the associated probability. This probability gives some\n  8 | kind of confidence on the prediction. This example demonstrates how to display\n  9 | how well calibrated the predicted probabilities are and how to calibrate an\n 10 | uncalibrated classifier.\n 11 | \n 12 | The experiment is performed on an artificial dataset for binary classification\n 13 | with 100,000 samples (1,000 of them are used for model fitting) with 20\n 14 | features. Of the 20 features, only 2 are informative and 10 are redundant. The\n 15 | first figure shows the estimated probabilities obtained with logistic\n 16 | regression, Gaussian naive Bayes, and Gaussian naive Bayes with both isotonic\n 17 | calibration and sigmoid calibration. The calibration performance is evaluated\n 18 | with Brier score, reported in the legend (the smaller the better). One can\n 19 | observe here that logistic regression is well calibrated while raw Gaussian\n 20 | naive Bayes performs very badly. This is because of the redundant features\n 21 | which violate the assumption of feature-independence and result in an overly\n 22 | confident classifier, which is indicated by the typical transposed-sigmoid\n 23 | curve.\n 24 | \n 25 | Calibration of the probabilities of Gaussian naive Bayes with isotonic\n 26 | regression can fix this issue as can be seen from the nearly diagonal\n 27 | calibration curve. Sigmoid calibration also improves the brier score slightly,\n 28 | albeit not as strongly as the non-parametric isotonic regression. This can be\n 29 | attributed to the fact that we have plenty of calibration data such that the\n 30 | greater flexibility of the non-parametric model can be exploited.\n 31 | \n 32 | The second figure shows the calibration curve of a linear support-vector\n 33 | classifier (LinearSVC). LinearSVC shows the opposite behavior as Gaussian\n 34 | naive Bayes: the calibration curve has a sigmoid curve, which is typical for\n 35 | an under-confident classifier. In the case of LinearSVC, this is caused by the\n 36 | margin property of the hinge loss, which lets the model focus on hard samples\n 37 | that are close to the decision boundary (the support vectors).\n 38 | \n 39 | Both kinds of calibration can fix this issue and yield nearly identical\n 40 | results. This shows that sigmoid calibration can deal with situations where\n 41 | the calibration curve of the base classifier is sigmoid (e.g., for LinearSVC)\n 42 | but not where it is transposed-sigmoid (e.g., Gaussian naive Bayes).\n 43 | \"\"\"\n 44 | print(__doc__)\n 45 | \n 46 | # Author: Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n 47 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n 48 | # License: BSD Style.\n 49 | \n 50 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ======================================\n  3 | Probability calibration of classifiers\n  4 | ======================================\n  5 | \n  6 | When performing classification you often want to predict not only\n  7 | the class label, but also the associated probability. This probability\n  8 | gives you some kind of confidence on the prediction. However, not all\n  9 | classifiers provide well-calibrated probabilities, some being over-confident\n 10 | while others being under-confident. Thus, a separate calibration of predicted\n 11 | probabilities is often desirable as a postprocessing. This example illustrates\n 12 | two different methods for this calibration and evaluates the quality of the\n 13 | returned probabilities using Brier's score\n 14 | (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brier_score).\n 15 | \n 16 | Compared are the estimated probability using a Gaussian naive Bayes classifier\n 17 | without calibration, with a sigmoid calibration, and with a non-parametric\n 18 | isotonic calibration. One can observe that only the non-parametric model is\n 19 | able to provide a probability calibration that returns probabilities close\n 20 | to the expected 0.5 for most of the samples belonging to the middle\n 21 | cluster with heterogeneous labels. This results in a significantly improved\n 22 | Brier score.\n 23 | \"\"\"\n 24 | print(__doc__)\n 25 | \n 26 | # Author: Mathieu Blondel <mathieu@mblondel.org>\n 27 | #         Alexandre Gramfort <alexandre.gramfort@telecom-paristech.fr>\n 28 | #         Balazs Kegl <balazs.kegl@gmail.com>\n 29 | #         Jan Hendrik Metzen <jhm@informatik.uni-bremen.de>\n 30 | # License: BSD Style.\n 31 | \n 32 | import numpy as np\n 33 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 34 | from matplotlib import cm\n 35 | \n 36 | from sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n 37 | from sklearn.naive_bayes import GaussianNB\n 38 | from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss\n 39 | from sklearn.calibration import CalibratedClassifierCV\n 40 | from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 41 | \n 42 | \n 43 | n_samples = 50000\n 44 | n_bins = 3  # use 3 bins for calibration_curve as we have 3 clusters here\n 45 | \n 46 | # Generate 3 blobs with 2 classes where the second blob contains\n 47 | # half positive samples and half negative samples. Probability in this\n 48 | # blob is therefore 0.5.\n 49 | centers = [(-5, -5), (0, 0), (5, 5)]\n 50 | X, y = make_blobs(n_samples=n_samples, centers=centers, shuffle=False,\n 51 |                   random_state=42)\n 52 | \n 53 | y[:n_samples // 2] = 0\n 54 | y[n_samples // 2:] = 1\n 55 | sample_weight = np.random.RandomState(42).rand(y.shape[0])\n 56 | \n 57 | # split train, test for calibration\n 58 | X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test, sw_train, sw_test = \\\n 59 |     train_test_split(X, y, sample_weight, test_size=0.9, random_state=42)\n 60 | \n 61 | # Gaussian Naive-Bayes with no calibration\n 62 | clf = GaussianNB()\n 63 | clf.fit(X_train, y_train)  # GaussianNB itself does not support sample-weights\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:_fit_calibrator`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n569 | def _fit_calibrator(clf, predictions, y, classes, method, sample_weight=None):\n570 |     \"\"\"Fit calibrator(s) and return a `_CalibratedClassifier`\n571 |     instance.\n572 | \n573 |     `n_classes` (i.e. `len(clf.classes_)`) calibrators are fitted.\n574 |     However, if `n_classes` equals 2, one calibrator is fitted.\n575 | \n576 |     Parameters\n577 |     ----------\n578 |     clf : estimator instance\n579 |         Fitted classifier.\n580 | \n581 |     predictions : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_classes) or (n_samples, 1) \\\n582 |                     when binary.\n583 |         Raw predictions returned by the un-calibrated base classifier.\n584 | \n585 |     y : array-like, shape (n_samples,)\n586 |         The targets.\n587 | \n588 |     classes : ndarray, shape (n_classes,)\n589 |         All the prediction classes.\n590 | \n591 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}\n592 |         The method to use for calibration.\n593 | \n594 |     sample_weight : ndarray, shape (n_samples,), default=None\n595 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n596 | \n597 |     Returns\n598 |     -------\n599 |     pipeline : _CalibratedClassifier instance\n600 |     \"\"\"\n601 |     Y = label_binarize(y, classes=classes)\n602 |     label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(classes)\n603 |     pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(clf.classes_)\n604 |     calibrators = []\n605 |     for class_idx, this_pred in zip(pos_class_indices, predictions.T):\n606 |         if method == \"isotonic\":\n607 |             calibrator = IsotonicRegression(out_of_bounds=\"clip\")\n608 |         elif method == \"sigmoid\":\n609 |             calibrator = _SigmoidCalibration()\n610 |         else:\n611 |             raise ValueError(\n612 |                 f\"'method' should be one of: 'sigmoid' or 'isotonic'. Got {method}.\"\n613 |             )\n614 |         calibrator.fit(this_pred, Y[:, class_idx], sample_weight)\n615 |         calibrators.append(calibrator)\n616 | \n617 |     pipeline = _CalibratedClassifier(clf, calibrators, method=method, classes=classes)\n618 |     return pipeline\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/calibration.py:_CalibratedClassifier`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n621 | class _CalibratedClassifier:\n622 |     \"\"\"Pipeline-like chaining a fitted classifier and its fitted calibrators.\n623 | \n624 |     Parameters\n625 |     ----------\n626 |     base_estimator : estimator instance\n627 |         Fitted classifier.\n628 | \n629 |     calibrators : list of fitted estimator instances\n630 |         List of fitted calibrators (either 'IsotonicRegression' or\n631 |         '_SigmoidCalibration'). The number of calibrators equals the number of\n632 |         classes. However, if there are 2 classes, the list contains only one\n633 |         fitted calibrator.\n634 | \n635 |     classes : array-like of shape (n_classes,)\n636 |         All the prediction classes.\n637 | \n638 |     method : {'sigmoid', 'isotonic'}, default='sigmoid'\n639 |         The method to use for calibration. Can be 'sigmoid' which\n640 |         corresponds to Platt's method or 'isotonic' which is a\n641 |         non-parametric approach based on isotonic regression.\n642 | \n643 |     Attributes\n644 |     ----------\n645 |     calibrators_ : list of fitted estimator instances\n646 |         Same as `calibrators`. Exposed for backward-compatibility. Use\n647 |         `calibrators` instead.\n648 | \n649 |         .. deprecated:: 0.24\n650 |            `calibrators_` is deprecated from 0.24 and will be removed in\n651 |            1.1 (renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\n652 |     \"\"\"\n653 | \n654 |     def __init__(self, base_estimator, calibrators, *, classes, method=\"sigmoid\"):\n655 |         self.base_estimator = base_estimator\n656 |         self.calibrators = calibrators\n657 |         self.classes = classes\n658 |         self.method = method\n659 | \n660 |     # TODO: Remove in 1.1\n661 |     # mypy error: Decorated property not supported\n662 |     @deprecated(  # type: ignore\n663 |         \"`calibrators_` is deprecated in 0.24 and will be removed in 1.1\"\n664 |         \"(renaming of 0.26). Use `calibrators` instead.\"\n665 |     )\n666 |     @property\n667 |     def calibrators_(self):\n668 |         return self.calibrators\n669 | \n670 |     def predict_proba(self, X):\n671 |         \"\"\"Calculate calibrated probabilities.\n672 | \n673 |         Calculates classification calibrated probabilities\n674 |         for each class, in a one-vs-all manner, for `X`.\n675 | \n676 |         Parameters\n677 |         ----------\n678 |         X : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n679 |             The sample data.\n680 | \n681 |         Returns\n682 |         -------\n683 |         proba : array, shape (n_samples, n_classes)\n684 |             The predicted probabilities. Can be exact zeros.\n685 |         \"\"\"\n686 |         n_classes = len(self.classes)\n687 |         pred_method, method_name = _get_prediction_method(self.base_estimator)\n688 |         predictions = _compute_predictions(pred_method, method_name, X, n_classes)\n689 | \n690 |         label_encoder = LabelEncoder().fit(self.classes)\n691 |         pos_class_indices = label_encoder.transform(self.base_estimator.classes_)\n692 | \n693 |         proba = np.zeros((_num_samples(X), n_classes))\n694 |         for class_idx, this_pred, calibrator in zip(\n695 |             pos_class_indices, predictions.T, self.calibrators\n696 |         ):\n697 |             if n_classes == 2:\n698 |                 # When binary, `predictions` consists only of predictions for\n699 |                 # clf.classes_[1] but `pos_class_indices` = 0\n700 |                 class_idx += 1\n701 |             proba[:, class_idx] = calibrator.predict(this_pred)\n702 | \n703 |         # Normalize the probabilities\n704 |         if n_classes == 2:\n705 |             proba[:, 0] = 1.0 - proba[:, 1]\n706 |         else:\n707 |             denominator = np.sum(proba, axis=1)[:, np.newaxis]\n708 |             # In the edge case where for each class calibrator returns a null\n709 |             # probability for a given sample, use the uniform distribution\n710 |             # instead.\n711 |             uniform_proba = np.full_like(proba, 1 / n_classes)\n712 |             proba = np.divide(\n713 |                 proba, denominator, out=uniform_proba, where=denominator != 0\n714 |             )\n715 | \n716 |         # Deal with cases where the predicted probability minimally exceeds 1.0\n717 |         proba[(1.0 < proba) & (proba <= 1.0 + 1e-5)] = 1.0\n718 | \n719 |         return proba\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:_sigmoid_calibration`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n722 | def _sigmoid_calibration(predictions, y, sample_weight=None):\n723 |     \"\"\"Probability Calibration with sigmoid method (Platt 2000)\n724 | \n725 |     Parameters\n726 |     ----------\n727 |     predictions : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n728 |         The decision function or predict proba for the samples.\n729 | \n730 |     y : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n731 |         The targets.\n732 | \n733 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n734 |         Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n735 | \n736 |     Returns\n737 |     -------\n738 |     a : float\n739 |         The slope.\n740 | \n741 |     b : float\n742 |         The intercept.\n743 | \n744 |     References\n745 |     ----------\n746 |     Platt, \"Probabilistic Outputs for Support Vector Machines\"\n747 |     \"\"\"\n748 |     predictions = column_or_1d(predictions)\n749 |     y = column_or_1d(y)\n750 | \n751 |     F = predictions  # F follows Platt's notations\n752 | \n753 |     # Bayesian priors (see Platt end of section 2.2)\n754 |     prior0 = float(np.sum(y <= 0))\n755 |     prior1 = y.shape[0] - prior0\n756 |     T = np.zeros(y.shape)\n757 |     T[y > 0] = (prior1 + 1.0) / (prior1 + 2.0)\n758 |     T[y <= 0] = 1.0 / (prior0 + 2.0)\n759 |     T1 = 1.0 - T\n760 | \n761 |     def objective(AB):\n762 |         # From Platt (beginning of Section 2.2)\n763 |         P = expit(-(AB[0] * F + AB[1]))\n764 |         loss = -(xlogy(T, P) + xlogy(T1, 1.0 - P))\n765 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n766 |             return (sample_weight * loss).sum()\n767 |         else:\n768 |             return loss.sum()\n769 | \n770 |     def grad(AB):\n771 |         # gradient of the objective function\n772 |         P = expit(-(AB[0] * F + AB[1]))\n773 |         TEP_minus_T1P = T - P\n774 |         if sample_weight is not None:\n775 |             TEP_minus_T1P *= sample_weight\n776 |         dA = np.dot(TEP_minus_T1P, F)\n777 |         dB = np.sum(TEP_minus_T1P)\n778 |         return np.array([dA, dB])\n779 | \n780 |     AB0 = np.array([0.0, log((prior0 + 1.0) / (prior1 + 1.0))])\n781 |     AB_ = fmin_bfgs(objective, AB0, fprime=grad, disp=False)\n782 |     return AB_[0], AB_[1]\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/calibration.py:_SigmoidCalibration`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n785 | class _SigmoidCalibration(RegressorMixin, BaseEstimator):\n786 |     \"\"\"Sigmoid regression model.\n787 | \n788 |     Attributes\n789 |     ----------\n790 |     a_ : float\n791 |         The slope.\n792 | \n793 |     b_ : float\n794 |         The intercept.\n795 |     \"\"\"\n796 | \n797 |     def fit(self, X, y, sample_weight=None):\n798 |         \"\"\"Fit the model using X, y as training data.\n799 | \n800 |         Parameters\n801 |         ----------\n802 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n803 |             Training data.\n804 | \n805 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n806 |             Training target.\n807 | \n808 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n809 |             Sample weights. If None, then samples are equally weighted.\n810 | \n811 |         Returns\n812 |         -------\n813 |         self : object\n814 |             Returns an instance of self.\n815 |         \"\"\"\n816 |         X = column_or_1d(X)\n817 |         y = column_or_1d(y)\n818 |         X, y = indexable(X, y)\n819 | \n820 |         self.a_, self.b_ = _sigmoid_calibration(X, y, sample_weight)\n821 |         return self\n822 | \n823 |     def predict(self, T):\n824 |         \"\"\"Predict new data by linear interpolation.\n825 | \n826 |         Parameters\n827 |         ----------\n828 |         T : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n829 |             Data to predict from.\n830 | \n831 |         Returns\n832 |         -------\n833 |         T_ : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n834 |             The predicted data.\n835 |         \"\"\"\n836 |         T = column_or_1d(T)\n837 |         return expit(-(self.a_ * T + self.b_))\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/calibration.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/calibration.py` for the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "After verifying the completeness of the found locations and analyzing their dependencies, I can confirm that the previously identified locations are indeed sufficient to implement the requested feature. Here's the final location-only format output with dependencies properly ordered by importance:\n\n```\nsklearn/calibration.py\nline: 840-945\nfunction: calibration_curve\ndependencies:\n- line: 840: function definition\n- line: 841-945: main calculation logic\n- line: 945: return values (prop_true, prop_pred)\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py\nclass: RocCurveDisplay\nfunction: plot\ndependencies:\n- implements similar plotting pattern for metrics\n- uses: ax parameter for visualization\n- handles default plot configuration\n- uses matplotlib for plotting\n\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py\nclass: PrecisionRecallDisplay\nfunction: plot\ndependencies:\n- implements similar plotting pattern for metrics\n- uses: ax parameter for visualization\n- handles default plot configuration\n- uses matplotlib for plotting\n\nsklearn/utils/__init__.py\nline: 1085-1102\nfunction: check_matplotlib_support\ndependencies:\n- imports matplotlib\n- ensures matplotlib availability\n- handles import errors\n```\n\nThis completes the verification of all necessary locations and their dependencies. The calibration curve plotting feature can be implemented by adding a new plotting function that:\n1. Uses the existing `calibration_curve` function to compute the calibration metrics\n2. Follows the plotting patterns from the metrics module\n3. Utilizes the existing matplotlib support infrastructure\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 225700, "completion_tokens": 994}, "time": 290.6954629421234}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-30128", "found_files": [["sklearn/metrics/_regression.py", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py"]], "found_modules": [["doc/sphinxext/sphinx_issues.py:IssueRole", "examples/gaussian_process/plot_gpr_on_structured_data.py:SequenceKernel", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/decomposition.py:MiniBatchDictionaryLearningBenchmark", "benchmarks/bench_sparsify.py:score", "examples/ensemble/plot_adaboost_multiclass.py:misclassification_error", "sklearn/utils/parallel.py:delayed", "examples/applications/plot_out_of_core_classification.py:ReutersParser", "doc/conftest.py:skip_if_cupy_not_installed", "examples/ensemble/plot_feature_transformation.py:gbdt_apply", "doc/sphinxext/dropdown_anchors.py:setup", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/_structures.py:NegativeInfinityType", "sklearn/utils/_mocking.py:MockDataFrame", "benchmarks/bench_tsne_mnist.py:sanitize", "doc/conftest.py:setup_grid_search", "examples/linear_model/plot_sgd_iris.py:plot_hyperplane", "examples/cluster/plot_adjusted_for_chance_measures.py:random_labels", "sklearn/utils/fixes.py:_object_dtype_isnan", "benchmarks/bench_sparsify.py:benchmark_dense_predict", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/common.py:Estimator", "sklearn/metrics/_regression.py:_check_reg_targets", "sklearn/metrics/pairwise.py:_argmin_reduce", "sklearn/utils/_metadata_requests.py:MetadataRequest", "sklearn/ensemble/_hist_gradient_boosting/grower.py:TreeGrower", "examples/miscellaneous/plot_metadata_routing.py:ExampleRegressor", "doc/conf.py:reset_sklearn_config", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_estimator_name", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_find_matching_floating_dtype", "build_tools/update_environments_and_lock_files.py:remove_from", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_array_api_dispatch", "sklearn/manifold/_isomap.py:Isomap", "sklearn/ensemble/_voting.py:VotingClassifier", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:NominalConversor", "sklearn/feature_extraction/_dict_vectorizer.py:DictVectorizer", "sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:_maybe