cheme passed onto `~.art3d.Poly3DCollection`\n3090 | \n3091 |         shade : bool, default: True\n3092 |             When true, this shades the dark sides of the bars (relative\n3093 |             to the plot's source of light).\n3094 | \n3095 |         lightsource : `~matplotlib.colors.LightSource`, optional\n3096 |             The lightsource to use when *shade* is True.\n3097 | \n3098 |         axlim_clip : bool, default: False\n3099 |             Whether to hide the bars with points outside the axes view limits.\n3100 | \n3101 |             .. versionadded:: 3.10\n3102 | \n3103 |         data : indexable object, optional\n3104 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n3105 | \n3106 |         **kwargs\n3107 |             Any additional keyword arguments are passed onto\n3108 |             `~.art3d.Poly3DCollection`.\n3109 | \n3110 |         Returns\n3111 |         -------\n3112 |         collection : `~.art3d.Poly3DCollection`\n3113 |             A collection of three-dimensional polygons representing the bars.\n3114 |         \"\"\"\n3115 | \n3116 |         had_data = self.has_data()\n3117 | \n3118 |         x, y, z, dx, dy, dz = np.broadcast_arrays(\n3119 |             np.atleast_1d(x), y, z, dx, dy, dz)\n3120 |         minx = np.min(x)\n3121 |         maxx = np.max(x + dx)\n3122 |         miny = np.min(y)\n3123 |         maxy = np.max(y + dy)\n3124 |         minz = np.min(z)\n3125 |         maxz = np.max(z + dz)\n3126 | \n3127 |         # shape (6, 4, 3)\n3128 |         # All faces are oriented facing outwards - when viewed from the\n3129 |         # outside, their vertices are in a counterclockwise ordering.\n3130 |         cuboid = np.array([\n3131 |             # -z\n3132 |             (\n3133 |                 (0, 0, 0),\n3134 |                 (0, 1, 0),\n3135 |                 (1, 1, 0),\n3136 |                 (1, 0, 0),\n3137 |             ),\n3138 |             # +z\n3139 |             (\n3140 |                 (0, 0, 1),\n3141 |                 (1, 0, 1),\n3142 |                 (1, 1, 1),\n3143 |                 (0, 1, 1),\n3144 |             ),\n3145 |             # -y\n3146 |             (\n3147 |                 (0, 0, 0),\n3148 |                 (1, 0, 0),\n3149 |                 (1, 0, 1),\n3150 |                 (0, 0, 1),\n3151 |             ),\n3152 |             # +y\n3153 |             (\n3154 |                 (0, 1, 0),\n3155 |                 (0, 1, 1),\n3156 |                 (1, 1, 1),\n3157 |                 (1, 1, 0),\n3158 |             ),\n3159 |             # -x\n3160 |             (\n3161 |                 (0, 0, 0),\n3162 |                 (0, 0, 1),\n3163 |                 (0, 1, 1),\n3164 |                 (0, 1, 0),\n3165 |             ),\n3166 |             # +x\n3167 |             (\n3168 |                 (1, 0, 0),\n3169 |                 (1, 1, 0),\n3170 |                 (1, 1, 1),\n3171 |                 (1, 0, 1),\n3172 |             ),\n3173 |         ])\n3174 | \n3175 |         # indexed by [bar, face, vertex, coord]\n3176 |         polys = np.empty(x.shape + cuboid.shape)\n3177 | \n3178 |         # handle each coordinate separately\n3179 |         for i, p, dp in [(0, x, dx), (1, y, dy), (2, z, dz)]:\n3180 |             p = p[..., np.newaxis, np.newaxis]\n3181 |             dp = dp[..., np.newaxis, np.newaxis]\n3182 |             polys[..., i] = p + dp * cuboid[..., i]\n3183 | \n3184 |         # collapse the first two axes\n3185 |         polys = polys.reshape((-1,) + polys.shape[2:])\n3186 | \n3187 |         facecolors = []\n3188 |         if color is None:\n3189 |             color = [self._get_patches_for_fill.get_next_color()]\n3190 | \n3191 |         color = list(mcolors.to_rgba_array(color))\n3192 | \n3193 |         if len(color) == len(x):\n3194 |             # bar colors specified, need to expand to number of faces\n3195 |             for c in color:\n3196 |                 facecolors.extend([c] * 6)\n3197 |         else:\n3198 |             # a single color specified, or face colors specified explicitly\n3199 |             facecolors = color\n3200 |             if len(facecolors) < len(x):\n3201 |                 facecolors *= (6 * len(x))\n3202 | \n3203 |         col = art3d.Poly3DCollection(polys,\n3204 |                                      zsort=zsort,\n3205 |                                      facecolors=facecolors,\n3206 |                                      shade=shade,\n3207 |                                      lightsource=lightsource,\n3208 |                                      axlim_clip=axlim_clip,\n3209 |                                      *args, **kwargs)\n3210 |         self.add_collection(col)\n3211 | \n3212 |         self.auto_scale_xyz((minx, maxx), (miny, maxy), (minz, maxz), had_data)\n3213 | \n3214 |         return col\n```\n\nFound class `lib/matplotlib/patches.py:ConnectionStyle.Bar`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3083 | class Bar(_Base):\n3084 |         \"\"\"\n3085 |         A line with *angle* between A and B with *armA* and *armB*. One of the\n3086 |         arms is extended so that they are connected in a right angle. The\n3087 |         length of *armA* is determined by (*armA* + *fraction* x AB distance).\n3088 |         Same for *armB*.\n3089 |         \"\"\"\n3090 | \n3091 |         def __init__(self, armA=0., armB=0., fraction=0.3, angle=None):\n3092 |             \"\"\"\n3093 |             Parameters\n3094 |             ----------\n3095 |             armA : float\n3096 |                 Minimum length of armA.\n3097 | \n3098 |             armB : float\n3099 |                 Minimum length of armB.\n3100 | \n3101 |             fraction : float\n3102 |                 A fraction of the distance between two points that will be\n3103 |                 added to armA and armB.\n3104 | \n3105 |             angle : float or None\n3106 |                 Angle of the connecting line (if None, parallel to A and B).\n3107 |             \"\"\"\n3108 |             self.armA = armA\n3109 |             self.armB = armB\n3110 |             self.fraction = fraction\n3111 |             self.angle = angle\n3112 | \n3113 |         def connect(self, posA, posB):\n3114 |             x1, y1 = posA\n3115 |             x20, y20 = x2, y2 = posB\n3116 | \n3117 |             theta1 = math.atan2(y2 - y1, x2 - x1)\n3118 |             dx, dy = x2 - x1, y2 - y1\n3119 |             dd = (dx * dx + dy * dy) ** .5\n3120 |             ddx, ddy = dx / dd, dy / dd\n3121 | \n3122 |             armA, armB = self.armA, self.armB\n3123 | \n3124 |             if self.angle is not None:\n3125 |                 theta0 = np.deg2rad(self.angle)\n3126 |                 dtheta = theta1 - theta0\n3127 |                 dl = dd * math.sin(dtheta)\n3128 |                 dL = dd * math.cos(dtheta)\n3129 |                 x2, y2 = x1 + dL * math.cos(theta0), y1 + dL * math.sin(theta0)\n3130 |                 armB = armB - dl\n3131 | \n3132 |                 # update\n3133 |                 dx, dy = x2 - x1, y2 - y1\n3134 |                 dd2 = (dx * dx + dy * dy) ** .5\n3135 |                 ddx, ddy = dx / dd2, dy / dd2\n3136 | \n3137 |             arm = max(armA, armB)\n3138 |             f = self.fraction * dd + arm\n3139 | \n3140 |             cx1, cy1 = x1 + f * ddy, y1 - f * ddx\n3141 |             cx2, cy2 = x2 + f * ddy, y2 - f * ddx\n3142 | \n3143 |             vertices = [(x1, y1),\n3144 |                         (cx1, cy1),\n3145 |                         (cx2, cy2),\n3146 |                         (x20, y20)]\n3147 |             codes = [Path.MOVETO,\n3148 |                      Path.LINETO,\n3149 |                      Path.LINETO,\n3150 |                      Path.LINETO]\n3151 | \n3152 |             return Path(vertices, codes)\n```\n\nFound function `lib/mpl_toolkits/mplot3d/axes3d.py:Axes3D.bar`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2989 | def bar(self, left, height, zs=0, zdir='z', *args,\n2990 |             axlim_clip=False, **kwargs):\n2991 |         \"\"\"\n2992 |         Add 2D bar(s).\n2993 | \n2994 |         Parameters\n2995 |         ----------\n2996 |         left : 1D array-like\n2997 |             The x coordinates of the left sides of the bars.\n2998 |         height : 1D array-like\n2999 |             The height of the bars.\n3000 |         zs : float or 1D array-like, default: 0\n3001 |             Z coordinate of bars; if a single value is specified, it will be\n3002 |             used for all bars.\n3003 |         zdir : {'x', 'y', 'z'}, default: 'z'\n3004 |             When plotting 2D data, the direction to use as z ('x', 'y' or 'z').\n3005 |         axlim_clip : bool, default: False\n3006 |             Whether to hide bars with points outside the axes view limits.\n3007 | \n3008 |             .. versionadded:: 3.10\n3009 |         data : indexable object, optional\n3010 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n3011 |         **kwargs\n3012 |             Other keyword arguments are forwarded to\n3013 |             `matplotlib.axes.Axes.bar`.\n3014 | \n3015 |         Returns\n3016 |         -------\n3017 |         mpl_toolkits.mplot3d.art3d.Patch3DCollection\n3018 |         \"\"\"\n3019 |         had_data = self.has_data()\n3020 | \n3021 |         patches = super().bar(left, height, *args, **kwargs)\n3022 | \n3023 |         zs = np.broadcast_to(zs, len(left), subok=True)\n3024 | \n3025 |         verts = []\n3026 |         verts_zs = []\n3027 |         for p, z in zip(patches, zs):\n3028 |             vs = art3d._get_patch_verts(p)\n3029 |             verts += vs.tolist()\n3030 |             verts_zs += [z] * len(vs)\n3031 |             art3d.patch_2d_to_3d(p, z, zdir, axlim_clip)\n3032 |             if 'alpha' in kwargs:\n3033 |                 p.set_alpha(kwargs['alpha'])\n3034 | \n3035 |         if len(verts) > 0:\n3036 |             # the following has to be skipped if verts is empty\n3037 |             # NOTE: Bugs could still occur if len(verts) > 0,\n3038 |             #       but the \"2nd dimension\" is empty.\n3039 |             xs, ys = zip(*verts)\n3040 |         else:\n3041 |             xs, ys = [], []\n3042 | \n3043 |         xs, ys, verts_zs = art3d.juggle_axes(xs, ys, verts_zs, zdir)\n3044 |         self.auto_scale_xyz(xs, ys, verts_zs, had_data)\n3045 | \n3046 |         return patches\n```\n\nFound class `lib/matplotlib/container.py:BarContainer`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n42 | class BarContainer(Container):\n43 |     \"\"\"\n44 |     Container for the artists of bar plots (e.g. created by `.Axes.bar`).\n45 | \n46 |     The container can be treated as a tuple of the *patches* themselves.\n47 |     Additionally, you can access these and further parameters by the\n48 |     attributes.\n49 | \n50 |     Attributes\n51 |     ----------\n52 |     patches : list of :class:`~matplotlib.patches.Rectangle`\n53 |         The artists of the bars.\n54 | \n55 |     errorbar : None or :class:`~matplotlib.container.ErrorbarContainer`\n56 |         A container for the error bar artists if error bars are present.\n57 |         *None* otherwise.\n58 | \n59 |     datavalues : None or array-like\n60 |         The underlying data values corresponding to the bars.\n61 | \n62 |     orientation : {'vertical', 'horizontal'}, default: None\n63 |         If 'vertical', the bars are assumed to be vertical.\n64 |         If 'horizontal', the bars are assumed to be horizontal.\n65 | \n66 |     \"\"\"\n67 | \n68 |     def __init__(self, patches, errorbar=None, *, datavalues=None,\n69 |                  orientation=None, **kwargs):\n70 |         self.patches = patches\n71 |         self.errorbar = errorbar\n72 |         self.datavalues = datavalues\n73 |         self.orientation = orientation\n74 |         super().__init__(patches, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound file `galleries/plot_types/basic/bar.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `galleries/plot_types/basic/bar.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"bar()\", \"matplotlib.bar\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py:Axes.bar`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2326 | def bar(self, x, height, width=0.8, bottom=None, *, align=\"center\",\n2327 |             **kwargs):\n2328 |         r\"\"\"\n2329 |         Make a bar plot.\n2330 | \n2331 |         The bars are positioned at *x* with the given *align*\\ment. Their\n2332 |         dimensions are given by *height* and *width*. The vertical baseline\n2333 |         is *bottom* (default 0).\n2334 | \n2335 |         Many parameters can take either a single value applying to all bars\n2336 |         or a sequence of values, one for each bar.\n2337 | \n2338 |         Parameters\n2339 |         ----------\n2340 |         x : float or array-like\n2341 |             The x coordinates of the bars. See also *align* for the\n2342 |             alignment of the bars to the coordinates.\n2343 | \n2344 |         height : float or array-like\n2345 |             The height(s) of the bars.\n2346 | \n2347 |             Note that if *bottom* has units (e.g. datetime), *height* should be in\n2348 |             units that are a difference from the value of *bottom* (e.g. timedelta).\n2349 | \n2350 |         width : float or array-like, default: 0.8\n2351 |             The width(s) of the bars.\n2352 | \n2353 |             Note that if *x* has units (e.g. datetime), then *width* should be in\n2354 |             units that are a difference (e.g. timedelta) around the *x* values.\n2355 | \n2356 |         bottom : float or array-like, default: 0\n2357 |             The y coordinate(s) of the bottom side(s) of the bars.\n2358 | \n2359 |             Note that if *bottom* has units, then the y-axis will get a Locator and\n2360 |             Formatter appropriate for the units (e.g. dates, or categorical).\n2361 | \n2362 |         align : {'center', 'edge'}, default: 'center'\n2363 |             Alignment of the bars to the *x* coordinates:\n2364 | \n2365 |             - 'center': Center the base on the *x* positions.\n2366 |             - 'edge': Align the left edges of the bars with the *x* positions.\n2367 | \n2368 |             To align the bars on the right edge pass a negative *width* and\n2369 |             ``align='edge'``.\n2370 | \n2371 |         Returns\n2372 |         -------\n2373 |         `.BarContainer`\n2374 |             Container with all the bars and optionally errorbars.\n2375 | \n2376 |         Other Parameters\n2377 |         ----------------\n2378 |         color : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2379 |             The colors of the bar faces.\n2380 | \n2381 |         edgecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2382 |             The colors of the bar edges.\n2383 | \n2384 |         linewidth : float or array-like, optional\n2385 |             Width of the bar edge(s). If 0, don't draw edges.\n2386 | \n2387 |         tick_label : str or list of str, optional\n2388 |             The tick labels of the bars.\n2389 |             Default: None (Use default numeric labels.)\n2390 | \n2391 |         label : str or list of str, optional\n2392 |             A single label is attached to the resulting `.BarContainer` as a\n2393 |             label for the whole dataset.\n2394 |             If a list is provided, it must be the same length as *x* and\n2395 |             labels the individual bars. Repeated labels are not de-duplicated\n2396 |             and will cause repeated label entries, so this is best used when\n2397 |             bars also differ in style (e.g., by passing a list to *color*.)\n2398 | \n2399 |         xerr, yerr : float or array-like of shape(N,) or shape(2, N), optional\n2400 |             If not *None*, add horizontal / vertical errorbars to the bar tips.\n2401 |             The values are +/- sizes relative to the data:\n2402 | \n2403 |             - scalar: symmetric +/- values for all bars\n2404 |             - shape(N,): symmetric +/- values for each bar\n2405 |             - shape(2, N): Separate - and + values for each bar. First row\n2406 |               contains the lower errors, the second row contains the upper\n2407 |               errors.\n2408 |             - *None*: No errorbar. (Default)\n2409 | \n2410 |             See :doc:`/gallery/statistics/errorbar_features` for an example on\n2411 |             the usage of *xerr* and *yerr*.\n2412 | \n2413 |         ecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, default: 'black'\n2414 |             The line color of the errorbars.\n2415 | \n2416 |         capsize : float, default: :rc:`errorbar.capsize`\n2417 |            The length of the error bar caps in points.\n2418 | \n2419 |         error_kw : dict, optional\n2420 |             Dictionary of keyword arguments to be passed to the\n2421 |             `~.Axes.errorbar` method. Values of *ecolor* or *capsize* defined\n2422 |             here take precedence over the independent keyword arguments.\n2423 | \n2424 |         log : bool, default: False\n2425 |             If *True*, set the y-axis to be log scale.\n2426 | \n2427 |         data : indexable object, optional\n2428 |             DATA_PARAMETER_PLACEHOLDER\n2429 | \n2430 |         **kwargs : `.Rectangle` properties\n2431 | \n2432 |         %(Rectangle:kwdoc)s\n2433 | \n2434 |         See Also\n2435 |         --------\n2436 |         barh : Plot a horizontal bar plot.\n2437 | \n2438 |         Notes\n2439 |         -----\n2440 |         Stacked bars can be achieved by passing individual *bottom* values per\n2441 |         bar. See :doc:`/gallery/lines_bars_and_markers/bar_stacked`.\n2442 |         \"\"\"\n2443 |         kwargs = cbook.normalize_kwargs(kwargs, mpatches.Patch)\n2444 |         color = kwargs.pop('color', None)\n2445 |         if color is None:\n2446 |             color = self._get_patches_for_fill.get_next_color()\n2447 |         edgecolor = kwargs.pop('edgecolor', None)\n2448 |         linewidth = kwargs.pop('linewidth', None)\n2449 |         hatch = kwargs.pop('hatch', None)\n2450 | \n2451 |         # Because xerr and yerr will be passed to errorbar, most dimension\n2452 |         # checking and processing will be left to the errorbar method.\n2453 |         xerr = kwargs.pop('xerr', None)\n2454 |         yerr = kwargs.pop('yerr', None)\n2455 |         error_kw = kwargs.pop('error_kw', None)\n2456 |         error_kw = {} if error_kw is None else error_kw.copy()\n2457 |         ezorder = error_kw.pop('zorder', None)\n2458 |         if ezorder is None:\n2459 |             ezorder = kwargs.get('zorder', None)\n2460 |             if ezorder is not None:\n2461 |                 # If using the bar zorder, increment slightly to make sure\n2462 |                 # errorbars are drawn on top of bars\n2463 |                 ezorder += 0.01\n2464 |         error_kw.setdefault('zorder', ezorder)\n2465 |         ecolor = kwargs.pop('ecolor', 'k')\n2466 |         capsize = kwargs.pop('capsize', mpl.rcParams[\"errorbar.capsize\"])\n2467 |         error_kw.setdefault('ecolor', ecolor)\n2468 |         error_kw.setdefault('capsize', capsize)\n2469 | \n2470 |         # The keyword argument *orientation* is used by barh() to defer all\n2471 |         # logic and drawing to bar(). It is considered internal and is\n2472 |         # intentionally not mentioned in the docstring.\n2473 |         orientation = kwargs.pop('orientation', 'vertical')\n2474 |         _api.check_in_list(['vertical', 'horizontal'], orientation=orientation)\n2475 |         log = kwargs.pop('log', False)\n2476 |         label = kwargs.pop('label', '')\n2477 |         tick_labels = kwargs.pop('tick_label', None)\n2478 | \n2479 |         y = bottom  # Matches barh call signature.\n2480 |         if orientation == 'vertical':\n2481 |             if y is None:\n2482 |                 y = 0\n2483 |         else:  # horizontal\n2484 |             if x is None:\n2485 |                 x = 0\n2486 | \n2487 |         if orientation == 'vertical':\n2488 |             # It is possible for y (bottom) to contain unit information.\n2489 |             # However, it is also possible for y=0 for the default and height\n2490 |             # to contain unit information.  This will prioritize the units of y.\n2491 |             self._process_unit_info(\n2492 |                 [(\"x\", x), (\"y\", y), (\"y\", height)], kwargs, convert=False)\n2493 |             if log:\n2494 |                 self.set_yscale('log', nonpositive='clip')\n2495 |         else:  # horizontal\n2496 |             # It is possible for x (left) to contain unit information.\n2497 |             # However, it is also possible for x=0 for the default and width\n2498 |             # to contain unit information.  This will prioritize the units of x.\n2499 |             self._process_unit_info(\n2500 |                 [(\"x\", x), (\"x\", width), (\"y\", y)], kwargs, convert=False)\n2501 |             if log:\n2502 |                 self.set_xscale('log', nonpositive='clip')\n2503 | \n2504 |         # lets do some conversions now since some types cannot be\n2505 |         # subtracted uniformly\n2506 |         if self.xaxis is not None:\n2507 |             x0 = x\n2508 |             x = np.asarray(self.convert_xunits(x))\n2509 |             width = self._convert_dx(width, x0, x, self.convert_xunits)\n2510 |             if xerr is not None:\n2511 |                 xerr = self._convert_dx(xerr, x0, x, self.convert_xunits)\n2512 |         if self.yaxis is not None:\n2513 |             y0 = y\n2514 |             y = np.asarray(self.convert_yunits(y))\n2515 |             height = self._convert_dx(height, y0, y, self.convert_yunits)\n2516 |             if yerr is not None:\n2517 |                 yerr = self._convert_dx(yerr, y0, y, self.convert_yunits)\n2518 | \n2519 |         x, height, width, y, linewidth, hatch = np.broadcast_arrays(\n2520 |             # Make args iterable too.\n2521 |             np.atleast_1d(x), height, width, y, linewidth, hatch)\n2522 | \n2523 |         # Now that units have been converted, set the tick locations.\n2524 |         if orientation == 'vertical':\n2525 |             tick_label_axis = self.xaxis\n2526 |             tick_label_position = x\n2527 |         else:  # horizontal\n2528 |             tick_label_axis = self.yaxis\n2529 |             tick_label_position = y\n2530 | \n2531 |         if not isinstance(label, str) and np.iterable(label):\n2532 |             bar_container_label = '_nolegend_'\n2533 |             patch_labels = label\n2534 |         else:\n2535 |             bar_container_label = label\n2536 |             patch_labels = ['_nolegend_'] * len(x)\n2537 |         if len(patch_labels) != len(x):\n2538 |             raise ValueError(f'number of labels ({len(patch_labels)}) '\n2539 |                              f'does not match number of bars ({len(x)}).')\n2540 | \n2541 |         linewidth = itertools.cycle(np.atleast_1d(linewidth))\n2542 |         hatch = itertools.cycle(np.atleast_1d(hatch))\n2543 |         color = itertools.chain(itertools.cycle(mcolors.to_rgba_array(color)),\n2544 |                                 # Fallback if color == \"none\".\n2545 |                                 itertools.repeat('none'))\n2546 |         if edgecolor is None:\n2547 |             edgecolor = itertools.repeat(None)\n2548 |         else:\n2549 |             edgecolor = itertools.chain(\n2550 |                 itertools.cycle(mcolors.to_rgba_array(edgecolor)),\n2551 |                 # Fallback if edgecolor == \"none\".\n2552 |                 itertools.repeat('none'))\n2553 | \n2554 |         # We will now resolve the alignment and really have\n2555 |         # left, bottom, width, height vectors\n2556 |         _api.check_in_list(['center', 'edge'], align=align)\n2557 |         if align == 'center':\n2558 |             if orientation == 'vertical':\n2559 |                 try:\n2560 |                     left = x - width / 2\n2561 |                 except TypeError as e:\n2562 |                     raise TypeError(f'the dtypes of parameters x ({x.dtype}) '\n2563 |                                     f'and width ({width.dtype}) '\n2564 |                                     f'are incompatible') from e\n2565 |                 bottom = y\n2566 |             else:  # horizontal\n2567 |                 try:\n2568 |                     bottom = y - height / 2\n2569 |                 except TypeError as e:\n2570 |                     raise TypeError(f'the dtypes of parameters y ({y.dtype}) '\n2571 |                                     f'and height ({height.dtype}) '\n2572 |                                     f'are incompatible') from e\n2573 |                 left = x\n2574 |         else:  # edge\n2575 |             left = x\n2576 |             bottom = y\n2577 | \n2578 |         patches = []\n2579 |         args = zip(left, bottom, width, height, color, edgecolor, linewidth,\n2580 |                    hatch, patch_labels)\n2581 |         for l, b, w, h, c, e, lw, htch, lbl in args:\n2582 |             r = mpatches.Rectangle(\n2583 |                 xy=(l, b), width=w, height=h,\n2584 |                 facecolor=c,\n2585 |                 edgecolor=e,\n2586 |                 linewidth=lw,\n2587 |                 label=lbl,\n2588 |                 hatch=htch,\n2589 |                 )\n2590 |             r._internal_update(kwargs)\n2591 |             r.get_path()._interpolation_steps = 100\n2592 |             if orientation == 'vertical':\n2593 |                 r.sticky_edges.y.append(b)\n2594 |             else:  # horizontal\n2595 |                 r.sticky_edges.x.append(l)\n2596 |             self.add_patch(r)\n2597 |             patches.append(r)\n2598 | \n2599 |         if xerr is not None or yerr is not None:\n2600 |             if orientation == 'vertical':\n2601 |                 # using list comps rather than arrays to preserve unit info\n2602 |                 ex = [l + 0.5 * w for l, w in zip(left, width)]\n2603 |                 ey = [b + h for b, h in zip(bottom, height)]\n2604 | \n2605 |             else:  # horizontal\n2606 |                 # using list comps rather than arrays to preserve unit info\n2607 |                 ex = [l + w for l, w in zip(left, width)]\n2608 |                 ey = [b + 0.5 * h for b, h in zip(bottom, height)]\n2609 | \n2610 |             error_kw.setdefault(\"label\", '_nolegend_')\n2611 | \n2612 |             errorbar = self.errorbar(ex, ey, yerr=yerr, xerr=xerr, fmt='none',\n2613 |                                      **error_kw)\n2614 |         else:\n2615 |             errorbar = None\n2616 | \n2617 |         self._request_autoscale_view()\n2618 | \n2619 |         if orientation == 'vertical':\n2620 |             datavalues = height\n2621 |         else:  # horizontal\n2622 |             datavalues = width\n2623 | \n2624 |         bar_container = BarContainer(patches, errorbar, datavalues=datavalues,\n2625 |                                      orientation=orientation,\n2626 |                                      label=bar_container_label)\n2627 |         self.add_container(bar_container)\n2628 | \n2629 |         if tick_labels is not None:\n2630 |             tick_labels = np.broadcast_to(tick_labels, len(patches))\n2631 |             tick_label_axis.set_ticks(tick_label_position)\n2632 |             tick_label_axis.set_ticklabels(tick_labels)\n2633 | \n2634 |         return bar_container\n```\n\nFound function `lib/matplotlib/pyplot.py:bar`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2969 | def bar(\n2970 |     x: float | ArrayLike,\n2971 |     height: float | ArrayLike,\n2972 |     width: float | ArrayLike = 0.8,\n2973 |     bottom: float | ArrayLike | None = None,\n2974 |     *,\n2975 |     align: Literal[\"center\", \"edge\"] = \"center\",\n2976 |     data=None,\n2977 |     **kwargs,\n2978 | ) -> BarContainer:\n2979 |     return gca().bar(\n2980 |         x,\n2981 |         height,\n2982 |         width=width,\n2983 |         bottom=bottom,\n2984 |         align=align,\n2985 |         **({\"data\": data} if data is not None else {}),\n2986 |         **kwargs,\n2987 |     )\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me analyze the code and identify the relevant locations for implementing the requested changes. Based on the issue description and code analysis, I'll focus on these key areas:\n\n1. The main bar plotting function in `_axes.py`\n2. The bar container class in `container.py`\n3. Related documentation files\n\nHere are the relevant locations that need to be modified to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\nlib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py\nline: 2378-2379\nline: 2381\nline: 2382\nline: 2443-2447\nline: 2543-2544\nclass: Axes\nfunction: bar\n\nlib/matplotlib/container.py\nline: 42\nline: 68\nclass: BarContainer\nfunction: __init__\n\nlib/matplotlib/colorbar.py\nline: 1\nfunction: normalize\n\nlib/galleries/plot_types/basic/bar.py\nline: 1\n```\n\nExplanation of why these locations are relevant:\n\n1. `lib/matplotlib/axes/_axes.py`:\n   - Lines 2378-2379: Current `color` parameter handling\n   - Lines 2381, 2382: Existing `edgecolor` parameter\n   - Lines 2443-2447: Color processing and normalization\n   - Lines 2543-2544: Color cycle implementation\n\n2. `lib/matplotlib/container.py`\n   - Class defining the bar container which handles the actual bar rendering\n   - Lines 42-69 in the `__init__` method control the initialization of bar properties\n\n3. `lib/matplotlib/colorbar.py`\n   - Contains the color normalization and conversion utilities needed for the improved color handling\n\n4. `galleries/plot_types/basic/bar.py`\n   - Documentation and examples for the bar plotting functionality\n\nThe main changes needed are:\n1. Modify the `bar()` function in `_axes.py` to make `facecolor` accept sequences\n2. Update the `BarContainer` class to properly handle color sequencing\n3. Ensure proper color parameter precedence between `color`, `edgecolor`, and `facecolor`\n4. Add color normalization and conversion utilities where needed\n5. Update documentation and examples to reflect the new behavior\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nNote:\n'<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n- executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n- tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n- then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\nArgs:\n- `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n- `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n- `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`. \n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n- `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n- `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n\nReturns:\nA JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n- `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n- `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n- `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n- `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n- `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n- `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n\nEach artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n- `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n- `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n- `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n- `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n- `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n- `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n- `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n- `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 28525, "completion_tokens": 547}, "time": 71.48341107368469}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-59900", "found_files": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py", "pandas/core/accessor.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py", "pandas/core/dtypes/__init__.py"]], "found_modules": [["asv_bench/benchmarks/inference.py:ToDatetimeCache", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods", "pandas/core/arrays/numpy_.py:NumpyExtensionArray", "pandas/core/indexes/datetimelike.py:DatetimeIndexOpsMixin", "pandas/core/accessor.py:Accessor", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py:is_dtype_equal", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:DatetimeTZDtype"]], "found_entities": [["asv_bench/benchmarks/inference.py:ToDatetimeCache.time_dup_string_dates", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate", "pandas/core/arrays/numpy_.py:NumpyExtensionArray.isna", "pandas/core/indexes/datetimelike.py:DatetimeIndexOpsMixin._formatter_func", "pandas/core/accessor.py:Accessor.__get__", "pandas/core/dtypes/common.py:is_dtype_equal", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:DatetimeTZDtype.str"]], "raw_output_loc": ["After a thorough analysis of the additional search results and trace artifacts, I can confirm that the initially found locations are complete and sufficient to address the issue. Let me present the final locations in the required format:\n\n```\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 257-259\nfunction: StringMethods._validate\n\npandas/core/accessor.py\nline: 224-228\nfunction: Accessor.__get__\n\npandas/core/dtypes/common.py\nline: 1\nfunction: is_dtype_equal\n\npandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py\nline: 1\nfunction: DatetimeTZDtype.str\n\npandas/core/dtypes/__init__.py\nline: 5\nfunction: infer_dtype\n```\n\nThe analysis shows that these locations cover all the essential components needed to implement the requested feature:\n\n1. `StringMethods._validate`: This is the primary location needing modification as it contains the current error message without dtype information.\n2. `Accessor.__get__`: Provides the base accessor functionality that propagates the error.\n3. `is_dtype_equal`: Used for type checking comparisons in the accessor system.\n4. `DatetimeTZDtype.str`: Reference implementation showing the pattern for formatting dtype information in error messages.\n5. `infer_dtype`: The core function used for type inference that provides the dtype information to include in the error message.\n\nThe issue is localized to the string accessor validation logic, and these locations encompass the complete chain of code needed to properly display the inferred dtype when a string accessor is used incorrectly."], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "8d2ca0bf84bcf44a800ac19bdb4ed7ec88c555e2", "problem_statement": "ENH: increase verbosity on error\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nindex 3cb0e75cfb815..05e1a36877e06 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ def _validate(data):\n         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n \n         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n-            raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n+            raise AttributeError(\n+                f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values, not {inferred_dtype}\"\n+            )\n         return inferred_dtype\n \n     def __getitem__(self, key):\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 165472, "completion_tokens": 904}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json' is generated through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis, which involves:\n    - executing two Python scripts describing the same process with (A.py) and without (B.py) the feature enabled.\n    - tracing their execution, obtain trace A and trace B as the execution traces of A.py and B.py, respectively.\n    - then subtracting the overlapping parts from trace A and save the rest in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'. Therefore it contains the non-overlapping artifacts that only appear in A.py, which are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n    Args:\n    - `artifact_type`: Optional exact filter for artifact definition type. It can be `function`, `method`, or `class`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `all` and does not filter by artifact type.\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path. For example, if you are interested in artifacts defined in files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by file path.\n    - `name_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name. For example, if you are interested in artifacts with `DataLoader` in their qualified names, you can set `name_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by qualified name.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched artifact.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice. It defaults to 8, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time. You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched artifacts.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `trace_summary`: Summary counts, including `unique_function_count`, `returned_count`, and `filtered_count`.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `artifacts`: A JSON array of matched artifacts.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched artifacts are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each artifact in `artifacts` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `qualified_name`: The qualified name of the artifact.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the artifact is defined.\n    - `definition_type`: The raw definition type from DaLIC, such as `function` or `class`.\n    - `defined_line`: The line number where the artifact is defined.\n    - `end_line`: The ending line number of the artifact, if available.\n    - `event_count`: The number of matching execution events in trace A.\n    - `call_count`: The number of calls observed in trace A.\n    - `max_call_depth`: The maximum observed call depth for this artifact.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n    - Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Read a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' and returns a short JSON summary.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies filters and pagination.\n    Note:\n    - '<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json' is generated by analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts in '<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json'.\n    - It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts, which can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n    Args:\n    - `symbol_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to symbols with `DataLoader` in their names, you can set `symbol_contains` to `DataLoader`.\n    Together with `scope_contains` and `path_contains`, this first selects a set of matching symbols; an edge is retained when either endpoint belongs to that set.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `scope_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n    The scope is represented as a dot-separated string of nested scopes, such as `src.utils.data_loader.DataLoader.__init__`.\n    You can set `scope_contains` to a substring of the scope to filter dependencies related to that scope.\n    For example, setting it to `DataLoader` would match the above scope and include its dependencies.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `path_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `path_contains`: Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n    For example, if you are interested in dependencies related to files under `src/utils`, you can set `path_contains` to `src/utils`.\n    Together with `symbol_contains` and `scope_contains`, this contributes to the symbol-matching step described above.\n    If not specified, it defaults to `None`\n    - `edge_type`: Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`. If not specified, it defaults to `None` and does not filter by edge type.\n    - `start`: Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges. It defaults to 0, meaning that the first page starts from the first matched edge.\n    - `limit`: Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice. It defaults to 12, which is a reasonable number for you to inspect at a time.\n    You can adjust it based on your preference and the typical number of matched edges.\n    - `path_limit`: Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include. A dependency path is a sequence of dependency edges connecting two symbols.\n    If there are more matched paths than this limit, only the first few unique compressed paths are returned.\n    Important behavior:\n    - If at least one symbol matches the combined `symbol_contains` / `scope_contains` / `path_contains` filters, only edges whose source or destination is in that matched symbol set are retained.\n    - If no symbol matches those filters, edge filtering falls back to `edge_type` plus pagination only; it does not return an empty edge list.\n    Returns:\n    A JSON string encoding an object with the following top-level fields:\n    - `instance_id`: The current DaLIC instance id.\n    - `dep_summary`: Summary counts, including retained file / symbol / edge counts and the returned / filtered edge counts.\n    - `applied_filters`: The effective filter and pagination values used for this call.\n    - `matched_symbols`: A compact JSON array of symbols related to the current page of results.\n    - `edges`: A JSON array of matched dependency edges.\n    - `paths`: A JSON array of compressed dependency-path strings related to the current page.\n    - `has_more`: Whether more matched edges are available after this slice.\n    - `next_start`: The pagination offset to use for the next slice.\n    Each symbol in `matched_symbols` has these fields:\n    - `kind`: The raw symbol kind from DaLIC, such as `func`, `class`, or similar labels.\n    - `symbol`: The symbol name.\n    - `scope`: The raw scope string associated with the symbol.\n    - `path`: The compact file path where the symbol is defined.\n    - `line`: The line number where the symbol is defined.\n    Each edge in `edges` is a JSON object with the following fields:\n    - `src`: A compact source-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `dst`: A compact target-symbol object with fields `kind`, `symbol`, `scope`, `path`, and `line`.\n    - `edge_type`: The raw dependency edge type, such as `arg`, `data`, or `ret`.\n    Usage suggestions:\n    - Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many comma